#
#            The failure this round is about does not look like a failure, and
#            that is the whole of why it lasted.
#
#            R98 moved the PDF render off the valuation event loop and onto the
#            report service, because pdfkit is synchronous and a 409A render is
#            436–661ms during which the API serves nobody. The hop has a wire
#            schema, `RenderBody`, and the schema carries caps the library it
#            wraps does not. A payload that outgrows one is a 422 — and a 422 is
#            not an error here, it is a *fallback*: `clients/reportRender.ts`
#            renders the identical bytes in-process instead, which is correct,
#            deliberate, and the thing that makes the offload a latency
#            dependency rather than an availability one. Correct PDF, correct
#            route, 200, and the half-second is quietly back. Nothing says so
#            except `report_render_total{mode="local"}`.
#
#            `historyChart` was over one of those caps. It emits one point per
#            prior valuation of the same client and `historyFor` bounds neither
#            end of it — the scope is `(partner_id, company_name)`, so the
#            series grows for the life of the relationship and never shrinks —
#            against a wire cap of 40 points. A client on its forty-first
#            valuation therefore lost the offload for *every* render of *every*
#            report, permanently, from that valuation onward. Measured end to
#            end rather than reasoned about: 40 points delegate, 41 answer
#            `too_big` at `summary.charts.2.points` and fall back. The
#            engagements it hits are the ones with the longest history, which
#            are the ones with the largest reports.
#
#            The cap is now cut at the producer, from the *old* end, because the
#            question a trend chart answers is how this conclusion compares with
#            the recent ones. The note states the omission — "the most recent 40
#            of 47 prior valuations … 7 earlier valuations are not plotted" —
#            rather than leaving a reader to take a truncated series for the
#            client's whole history. A schedule that silently drops rows is this
#            platform's recurring defect and a signed report is the worst place
#            for it.
#
#            The census written for it caught two more. `approachChart` and
