# R329 — Observability & monitoring (M11). Four findings, and one shape running
#            through three of them: a failure whose *answer* is a success. R321
#            found the sweeps that fail by returning normally; this round found
#            the doors that fail by answering 4xx, 302, and 0.
#
#            **A rotated webhook secret refused every delivery, silently.** The
#            three inbound webhook doors are deliberately unauthenticated and
#            deliberately trusted; the whole of the authority behind each is a
#            signature, checked against a secret held on two machines neither of
#            which tells the other when it changes. `webhookSignatureCensus`
#            proved the check exists. Nothing proved its failure was visible. A
#            rotation in the Stripe dashboard that misses this deployment
#            refuses every delivery with `400 Invalid Stripe signature`; Stripe
#            retries for days and gives up; no payment is fulfilled, no refund
#            recorded, no subscription changes state, and a customer who has
#            been charged has an engagement that is still unpaid. The mail
#            provider's door drops every bounce and complaint the same way.
#            `registerProblemHandler` leaves 4xx unlogged on purpose — "those
#            describe the request, the caller was told, and logging them is
#            logging other people's mistakes" — which is right for a browser and
#            wrong for the one caller that is a machine, where the 4xx is ours
#            and the caller is the only party that can see it.
#            `inbound_webhook_deliveries_total{source,outcome}` counts every
#            delivery, `accepted` included, because a refusal count with no
#            denominator cannot separate a scanner from a secret that has been
#            wrong since Tuesday. `unsigned` and `bad_signature` are separate
#            labels and only the second is logged or alerted on: these URLs are
#            in `PUBLIC_ROUTES` and the internet scans them, and a scanner must
#            not be able to page somebody. The census that finds these doors by
#            their raw-buffer parser now requires each to count what it refused
#            and what it accepted.
#
#            **Five rules grouped by a label their metrics do not carry.** The
#            third direction of `alertRulesCensus`: it held every rule to a
#            metric this repo exports (R313) and to a label *value* something
#            sets (R321), and never to a label *name*. Five rules grouped and
#            annotated by `service` — which lives on `upstream_*`, where it names
#            the dependency being *called*, and on `n409_build_info`, and on
#            none of the process or HTTP instruments those five are written
#            against. `sum by (service) (rate(http_request_errors_total[5m]))`
#            drops `job` and `instance` and groups by a label that is not there,
#            so `HighServerErrorRate` — a page — fired on one series holding the
#            whole estate: the report unit is low-volume by design and could
#            fail every request it received while the web service's healthy
#            traffic held the quotient under five per cent. `SlowRequests` was
#            one p95 across the estate for the same reason. And
#            `{{ $labels.service }}` on a series without one renders empty, so
#            the three pages that could still fire named nobody — "  has
#            restarted repeatedly in the last ten minutes", on an estate of five
#            units. `job` is what the scraper attaches and the only label that
#            says which unit emitted a series; `ServiceDown` already relied on
#            it. The census now holds every label a rule groups by, selects on
#            or interpolates to one the metrics in that expression carry, or one
#            of the three the scraper and PromQL supply.
#
#            **A queue nobody was watching reported a confident zero.**
#            `evaluateJobAlerts` skips a source whose rule is disabled or
#            missing, and `reconcileJobAlerts` then resolves whatever was open
#            for it — so an unwatched queue produced no findings and published
#            the same row of zeros a healthy watched one does, and
#            `JobQueueAlertOpen` could never fire for it again. Worse than the
#            missing series R321 fixed: an absent series at least cannot be
#            mistaken for an answer. Not a corner, either — the comment on
#            `auto_pipeline_runs_pending` sends every DB-backed backlog to this
#            sweep, so for the outbox and the webhook deliveries that zero was
#            the whole of the monitoring. The scan keeps the rule state per
#            source beside the open set, `job_queue_alert_open` publishes only
#            for queues something is watching, and `job_queue_alert_rule
#            {source,state}` explains the absence — which is what makes leaving
