# R330 — Performance & efficiency (M8). Five findings, and the two largest are
#            the same shape from opposite ends: a cost paid per *unit* — per
#            entry, per cell, per navigation — where the work only had to be
#            done once. The measurements are all from this box and are quoted
#            below rather than characterised, because "expensive" is what let
#            three of these sit unread in comments that had already looked at
#            the code and concluded the opposite.
#
#            **The evidence bundle shipped twenty JSON documents uncompressed.**
#            `buildZip` stored every entry, and said why: "the bundle is mostly
#            JSON + already compressed PDFs, and auditors care about fidelity,
#            not bytes". The second half is true of exactly one entry — the
#            rendered report — and the first half is the argument for
#            compressing rather than against it. A bundle is twenty-odd
#            pretty-printed JSON documents of repeated record shapes, which is
#            about as compressible as anything gets: a real `calculations.json`
#            of 21 engine runs is 2.67 MB stored and 74.6 kB deflated — 2.8%, a
#            36x reduction, for 10.6 ms. `calculation-traces.json` is the
#            engine's whole working state per run and compresses harder still.
#            Fidelity was never the trade: deflate is lossless, and the CRC and
#            the uncompressed size in both headers describe the original bytes.
#            The same writer builds every .xlsx this platform exports — an .xlsx
#            *is* a ZIP of XML parts — so the workbook download was shipping raw
#            XML too, and that is the far more frequent of the two.
#            `domain/zipReader.ts` already reads method 8, which is what keeps a
#            workbook this writes parseable by the importer opposite it. Decided
#            per entry by trying it rather than by filename, so the PDF — which
#            comes back a shade larger — stays stored and the archive is never
#            bigger than it was. `crc32` went with it: `for (const byte of buf)`
#            allocates through the generic iteration protocol once per byte over
#            every byte of every entry, 21.8 ms against 6.2 ms indexed.
#
#            **A money formatter built per cell.** `Intl.NumberFormat` is
#            expensive to construct and cheap to call — 363 ms to
#            build-and-format twenty thousand values, 6.9 ms through one
#            instance, a factor of fifty-three. Every function in
#            `lib/format.ts` built a fresh one per call, across twenty-five call
#            sites, and these are per-cell functions: a two-hundred-class cap
#            table with half a dozen money columns is thousands of constructions
#            per render and does all of it again on the next one. `formatCents`
#            paid it twice per cell, once to read the currency's minor-unit
#            scale off `resolvedOptions` and once to render. Keyed by (currency,
#            options), which is the half a coarser cache gets wrong:
#            `formatAmount` widens the fraction digits below one unit, so a
#            $0.0001 par value and a $1,000 preference must not share an
#            instance. `lib/pipeline.ts` held a second copy of the same cost and
#            now routes through the same cache — picking up, as it goes, the
#            RangeError guard that call site predated.
#
#            **Every navigation re-asked three badge endpoints that could not
#            have changed.** R154's open item, closed. The three `AppLayout`
#            badge hooks each carried `location.pathname`, so a client-side
#            navigation fired all three immediately; walking five screens is
#            fifteen requests in a couple of seconds returning the numbers the
#            last one returned. R314 declined to touch the dependency and was
#            right to — the refetch-on-navigation is the point — and named what
#            was needed: a minimum interval. The interval is not a guess. It is
#            `UNREAD_COUNT_CACHE_TTL_MS` and `COUNTS_CACHE_TTL_MS`, the 15s TTL
#            caches all three endpoints already sit behind, so inside that
#            window the server *cannot* answer differently. Those caches were
#            added to absorb this exact storm, and `routes/inbox.ts` says so —
#            but a cache absorbs the query, not the request, and each of these
#            still costs a JWT verification, the `findAuthPrincipal` join every
#            authenticated route makes, and a rate-limit charge. This is the
#            other half of that fix, on the side doing the asking. The floor is
#            per hook instance and applies to navigation only, so a fresh mount
#            — reload, sign-in, first render — always polls.
#
#            **Every QA review pulled twenty engine request payloads for one key
#            of one of them.** `routes/qa.ts` reads the twenty-run window to
