# R341 — Observability & monitoring (M11). Five findings, and four of them are
#            the same question asked at four altitudes: *who reads this?* An
#            instrument computed and wired to nothing; a rule absent rather than
#            wrong; a refusal answered as a redirect; a ceiling that turned
#            itself off. Every one of them produces a system that looks healthy
#            from every channel an operator has.
#
#            **The two sweeps that report having examined nothing report it to
#            no rule.** `auto-email` and `job-alerts` each take a Postgres
#            session advisory lock inside the tick and decline when another pass
#            holds it. A declined pass returns exactly the zeros a pass that ran
#            and found nothing due returns — which is why each sweep flattens the
#            decline into its tally, `declined` (R340) and `skipped` (R321), for
#            the sole purpose of separating "did not happen" from "nothing to
#            do". Both fields reach `background_sweep_items_total`, and no rule
#            read either. Every rule in the background group is blind to the
#            condition: the tick ran, so `SweepStopped` is quiet; it did not
#            throw, so `SweepFailing` is quiet; it did not overlap *in-process*
#            — `SweepOverlapping` watches `nonOverlapping`, a different guard —
#            so that is quiet; and it failed no rows, so `SweepWorkFailing` is
#            quiet. The case both fields were written for is the permanent one:
#            `withSweepLock`'s own note describes a failed unlock returning a
#            live connection to the pool while it still holds a session-scoped
#            key, after which every later pass declines for ever and the only
#            line written for it is the benign `info`. Two rules, not one with
#            `outcome=~"declined|skipped"`, because the sweeps do not agree on
#            the spelling and `auto-email` *also* reports a `skipped` —
#            candidates passed over for want of a phone number, an entirely
#            ordinary outcome a regex across both would page on.
#            `JobMonitorNeverRuns` is a page rather than a ticket:
#            `job_queue_alert_open` is read from the last scan's snapshot, so a
#            monitor that has stopped scanning does not go absent, it keeps
#            publishing the verdict it reached before it stopped, and
#            `JobQueueAlertOpen` then reports zero open alerts for every queue
#            with total confidence. A fifth census direction now stands beside
#            the four in `alertRulesCensus.test.ts`: the four hold a rule that is
#            *wrong* (a metric nothing exports, a label value nothing sets, a
#            label name nothing carries, a divisor whose zero is not a
#            measurement), this holds the rule that is *absent*, which is harder
#            to notice because nothing in `alerts.yml` mentions it. `x ? 1 : 0`
#            in a sweep tally is the whole idiom — `sweepTally` counts numbers
#            only — so the third one is caught the day it is written.
#
#            **The registry's own degradation signal had no reader.** Every
#            instrument is capped at 200 distinct label sets and folds everything
#            past that into one reserved `__other__` series. `Family` has
#            computed `cardinality` and `truncated` since the cap was written and
#            the only thing that has ever read either is `prometheus.test.ts` —
#            so the event the module header names as the cost of the cap
#            ("attribution is what degrades, which is the right thing to lose")
#            happens permanently, per process, and reaches no channel at all. A
#            dashboard grouped by `route` starts showing `__other__` beside the
#            real routes as if it were one of them. The loss is not cosmetic in
#            the direction that matters: totals stay exact, which is why
#            `HighServerErrorRate` and `SlowRequests` are untouched — they all
#            `sum by (job)` — but a rule that *selects* a label value no longer
#            matches the folded readings, and `WebhookOutcomesUnrecorded`,
#            `JobAlertAnnouncementsFailing`, `SweepAbandoningWork`,
#            `ScimThrottled` and every SSO rule are that shape. `seriesCensus()`
#            plus two gauges in `registerProcessMetrics`, so all three Node
#            services get them without new wiring. `MetricAttributionFolded`
#            excludes `http_request*`, and that exclusion is the whole
#            calibration: `route` comes from a URL path and this estate serves
#            over four hundred of them, so those three are *expected* to fold,
#            while every other instrument carries a closed label domain small
#            enough that a fold is label values nobody declared.
#
#            **A gauge that throws when scraped left nothing behind.**
#            `ObservableGauge.render` caught its own `collect` and returned no
#            lines. Dropping the gauge is right and stays — an endpoint that
#            500s because a pool handle went away is down exactly when it is
#            needed — but an absent series is the one reading a rule cannot tell
#            apart from a healthy one, and nearly every page-severity rule here
#            is gauge-backed: `SweepStopped` reads `background_sweep_enabled`,
#            `UpstreamCircuitOpen` reads `upstream_circuit_state`,
#            `PoolSaturated` reads `db_pool_connections_waiting`,
#            `MemoryCeilingMissing` reads the cgroup. These collects are not
#            trivial — a live pool, a sweep roster, a scan snapshot, cgroup
#            files — and one that throws every scrape takes its rule with it for
#            as long as the process lives. The catch moves up to
#            `MetricsRegistry.render`, the only place that can record it; gauges
#            are collected before the counters are written out, though still
#            emitted last, so the increment lands in the same body as the
#            failure. Registered lazily: a permanent zero is a series an operator
#            learns to ignore.
#
#            **Three of the five ways an OAuth connect ends left no record.**
#            Each of the three callbacks — HRIS, accounting, cap-table sync —
#            builds one `back(result)` helper and answers every outcome through
#            it as a redirect. Five results go through it and two left a trace:
#            `connected` writes an audit event, `error` writes a warn for the
#            token exchange. `denied`, `retired` and `unauthorized` wrote nothing
#            at all, on any of the three doors. Which is `ssoOutcomes.ts`'s shape
#            one flow over: a refusal answered as a 302 is counted in
#            `http_requests_total`'s 3xx class beside every ordinary navigation,
#            no 5xx for `HighServerErrorRate`, nothing for `SlowRequests`, and no
#            breaker because the provider is not one of ours. A rotated Rippling
#            client secret, a redirect URI that no longer matches, or a role
#            migration that breaks `isOps` refuses every connection attempt with
#            every instrument on the box green, and the only symptom is a query
#            parameter in one person's browser. Recorded inside `back` rather
#            than at the five `return` sites — the `scheduleSweep` argument, and
#            it matters more here because the sixth outcome anybody adds will be
#            a refusal. `retired` and `unauthorized` also get a line, because the
#            counter is what a rule reads and the line is where the identities
#            are: `unauthorized` is a thirty-minute token presented by an account
#            whose access ended inside those thirty minutes, a security-relevant
#            refusal of a stale credential that `registerProblemHandler`
#            structurally cannot see.
#
#            **A mistyped response ceiling turned the ceiling off.** R340 left
#            this as a gap and it is closed. `max_response_bytes` had two silent
#            fallbacks and the AI tier already has the convention for both:
#            `limits._misconfigured` says "falling back *without saying so* is
#            its own failure", and `ratelimit.install_rate_limit` warns whenever
#            its ceiling resolves to nothing. An unparseable `16MB` took the
#            default in silence. And `max(value, 0)` resolves every negative to
#            0, which the function documents as off — `_CappedStream` tests
#            `self._limit > 0` — so a mistyped `-1` does not tighten the ceiling,
#            it removes the guard whose own header says "the failure it guards is
#            unbounded rather than merely large", after which the socket delivers
#            into a buffer nobody is draining for as long as the far end keeps
#            sending. Announced once per distinct reading rather than per call:
#            `new_client` builds a client per outbound request.
