# R342 — State machine & lifecycle (M3). Five findings, and three of them are
#            one flag asked at three doors. `partners.archived_at` is the
#            platform's soft delete for a firm; the doors a *person* uses were
#            each closed as somebody noticed them, and the doors with no person
#            behind them were never asked. The other two are the same shape one
#            subject over: a suspension every predicate subtracts except the
#            surface that consults no principal, and a reset link bound to an
#            account rather than to the address it was delivered to.
#
#            **An archived firm's API key kept every authority the firm had.**
#            `LIVE_LINK_SQL` in `repos/clientIntake.ts` states the rule without
#            hedging — "an archived partner takes no new user assignments, cannot
#            have its branding edited, and is gone from the branding list" — and
#            closed the intake links on the strength of it. `resolveApiToken`
#            asked whether the token was revoked, whether its owner still
#            existed, and whether that owner was still a member of
#            `t.partner_id`. It never asked whether `t.partner_id` was still a
#            firm. So a withdrawn firm's integration went on running unattended
#            against `/api/partner/v1`: listing its old clients, reading their
#            cap tables and concluded 409As, creating engagements under the
#            archived firm, uploading documents to them. One endpoint of the
#            fifteen had noticed — `GET /me` 404s an archived organisation, under
#            a comment reading "`apiKeyGuard` cannot catch this — it resolves the
#            token, and the token is fine" — which is a guard on the one endpoint
#            that reports an identity and on none of the fourteen that move
#            client data. The question moved to the credential layer as
#            `partner_retired`, a fifth `ApiTokenRefusal`, because the remedy is
#            unlike every other one on that list: minting a replacement under a
#            current member produces another key the same clause refuses. Refused
#            rather than revoked — archiving is a boolean an administrator can
#            set back — and the test asserts that round trip, not only the
#            refusal.
#
#            **A withdrawn firm went on being told about its old clients.**
#            `enabledWebhooks` is the one read all four fan-out call sites go
#            through, and its whole liveness test was `enabled`, which is the
#            firm's own switch. Three single-row callers ride every transition of
#            a partner engagement and the retention sweep fans out over a batch,
#            so the platform kept POSTing an archived firm's old clients'
#            engagement numbers, company names and states to a URL it owns, for
#            as long as those engagements moved — and the firm could not stop it,
#            because the console it would delete the hook from is behind a
#            partner API key, which the finding above now refuses. Only the
#            fan-out: deliveries already queued keep their retry ladder, because
#            the disclosure was made when the row was written and a claim query
#            taught to skip them would leave `pending` rows nothing ever settles.
#
#            **The one work queue that offered withdrawn engagements, and forbade
#            clearing them.** Every list building its own WHERE over `valuations`
#            has been swept into agreement with `archived_at` — the reviewer
#            sign-off queue and the pay-now list, the two remediation queues, the
#            shared inbox and its badge, the re-filing queue, the monitor scan,
#            the drip campaigns, the SLA sweep, the portfolio roll-up.
#            `review_tasks` never joined `valuations` at all. Worse here than in
#            the queues those rounds finished, because `refuseIfSubjectRetired`
#            reaches a task through its own id: moving one of these to `done` or
#            `cancelled` is answered 409, so the rows were permanent — not
#            workable, not clearable, `open` past their `due_at` forever, and
#            `overdue` computed from exactly that. An EXISTS filtered above
#            0193's console ordering rather than a join, which would invite the
#            planner to drive from `valuations` and sort the whole queue; the
#            plan is pinned beside 0193's own assertions.
#
#            **Suspending an account left its API key the firm's whole book.**
#            `ignored` subtracts — `valuationScope` answers `none`, `isOps` and
#            `canManageUsers` answer false, and R211 swept the four predicates
#            that had not been subtracting for themselves. Every one of those is
#            a question asked of a *principal*, and the partner API is the one
#            surface that never asks: `loadScoped` compares `partner_id` with
#            `token.partnerId` and the listing hand-builds `{ kind: 'partner' }`
#            rather than calling `valuationScope`. Deliberate, and documented —
#            an integration must not break because a seat's roles were edited —
#            but the exemption swallowed the suspension with it, so a suspended
#            org admin's key kept the firm's entire book and wrote the suspended
#            account's id onto the audit spine as the actor of each change. In
#            `apiKeyGuard` rather than in `resolveApiToken`, because asking about
#            a role there means a third and fourth SQL spelling of
