# R345 — Observability & monitoring (M11). Four findings. Two are an instrument
#            with no reader, one is a reader with the wrong audience, and the
#            fourth is what the third made true.
#
#            **The kernel memory events written as the early warning warned
#            nobody.** `cgroupMemory.ts` reads cgroup v2's `memory.events` and
#            exports its five counters with an argument for why they are the ones
#            worth having: "`high` counts times the cgroup was throttled at
#            `MemoryHigh`, `max` counts times an allocation was about to breach
#            `MemoryMax` and reclaim was forced ... the first two are the early
#            warning; they tick long before anything dies". `infra/DEPLOYMENT.md`
#            repeats it — "both tick long before anything dies and are the actual
#            early warning". All three Node units register the family. No rule in
#            `alerts.yml` named the metric at all.
#
#            `MemoryNearCgroupLimit` does not cover it and is not meant to. That
#            rule samples an instantaneous ratio and requires ten consecutive
#            minutes of it, so it sees a unit that grows into its ceiling and
#            sits there. The failure this estate actually has is the other shape
#            — a report render or a workbook import allocating hard inside one
#            request — where the cgroup forces reclaim and the request or the
#            unit dies between two fifteen-second scrapes, with the ratio never
#            observed above 0.9 for ten minutes running. The events counters are
#            monotonic within a process lifetime, so one scrape after the event
#            sees it; that is the whole difference between an instrument that
#            catches this and one that does not.
#
#            Three rules: `max` (page, the kill is next), `high` (ticket, the
#            sizing conversation) and `oom_kill` (page) for the case nothing else
#            can catch — the cgroup OOM handler picks a *process*, not a service,
#            so a killed child leaves the unit up and healthy on every other
#            instrument. `increase(...)` for the first two rather than `> 0`,
#            because the counters reset only on restart and a bare comparison
#            holds the alert open for a month after one touch at boot.
#            `alertRulesCensus.test.ts` gains a sixth direction held against the
#            exported `CGROUP_MEMORY_EVENTS`, so an event added to that constant
#            arrives with the case red until somebody decides whether it is worth
#            waking for; `low` and `oom` are excluded and argued in place.
#
#            **The last machine door refused integrations with nothing anywhere
#            saying so.** Every other machine-facing door on this platform was
#            instrumented in turn and for the same reason — inbound webhooks and
#            both SSO flows in R329, the directory connector in R337, the three
#            OAuth connect callbacks in R341 — and the argument each time is
#            `scimRequests.ts`'s: `registerProblemHandler` leaves 4xx unlogged on
#            purpose, which is right for a browser and wrong for the one caller
#            that is a machine and cannot tell anybody it is being turned away.
#
#            The API key was the door left, and it is the one this estate itself
#            calls "the door with no person behind it to notice". `plugins/auth.ts`
#            answered a refused key with one `problems.unauthorized(...)`: no log
#            line, no event row, a 401 in the same 4xx class as every mistyped
#            password on the box. A firm's integration stopped dead, the firm
#            could not find out why — for `orphaned` and `partner_retired` the
#            console it would look in is behind the same key — and this side had
#            nothing saying it had happened.
#
#            R342 is what makes that urgent rather than tidy. Three of the five
#            refusals the credential layer can now give are conditions this
#            platform caused: an administrator archived the firm, moved the
#            member who minted the key out of it, or closed their account. Each
