# R346 — Adversarial input & fence audit (M6). Four findings. Two are a
#            character the database will not take reaching a column through a
#            door the boundary guard does not cover; two are two readers of one
#            file disagreeing about what it says.
#
#            **A NUL from an upstream lost the whole diagnostic row.**
#            `boundedJson` makes one promise about the write — that what it
#            returns will insert into `network_items.request`/`.response` — and
#            R217 made that true for an unpaired surrogate in a string *value*.
#            Two ways past it were left. `U+0000` fails a jsonb insert for the
#            same reason and with the same silence: `JSON.stringify` emits
#            `\u0000`, Postgres's JSON parser has no text representation for that
#            escape, and `recordNetworkItem` swallows the error by design. And a
#            *key* is as unstorable as a value — `findUnstorableText` searches
#            both — while this walk rebuilt the object with the upstream's
#            spelling of every key intact.
#
#            `domain/nulBytes.ts` refuses both characters at the request door,
#            which is why neither showed up: this function's material is the
#            *response* of every engine and AI call, and a response never came
#            through that door. A model emitting a NUL inside a JSON string, or
#            a connector pull carrying one through from a client's own
#            spreadsheet, took out the trace on exactly the failure path the
#            trace exists for. `storable` now covers both characters and runs
#            over keys as well as values; the client-body membership test and its
#            marker keep reading the spelling that was sent.
#
#            **One NUL in a bounce message put the delivery webhook in a
#            permanent retry loop.** The three inbound webhook doors are the one
#            shape of route that steps outside `app.ts`'s global `preValidation`
#            scan. That hook is global because the exposure is per-column rather
#            than per-route — but it needs a parsed body, and a signature has to
#            be verified over the bytes that arrived, so each of these scopes
#            hands Fastify a raw Buffer, the hook walks past it, and the object
#            only exists inside the handler. `parseStripeEvent` knows this and
#            scans its own envelope, answering 400.
#            `POST /api/v1/webhooks/email/:provider` was written to the same
#            pattern without that half.
#
#            The cost is not a dropped row, which is what makes it worth a round.
#            `detail` is free text a provider copies out of a DSN; the character
#            fails the `email_delivery_events` insert, the per-event `catch` puts
#            the message in `unrecorded`, and `unrecorded` is answered 503
#            "redeliver this batch". So the provider redelivers, the same
#            character fails the same insert, and the batch is in a loop nothing
#            in it can leave — the good events re-applying idempotently forever
#            and the bad one never landing. `webhookSignatureCensus` gains the
#            rule for the next author to inherit: a raw-body scope must scan the
#            object it parsed.
#
#            **A two-line column title made a European CSV import as one
#            column.** `sniffDelimiter` cut the header at the first newline in
#            the *file*. A header whose own first cell is quoted and holds a line
#            break — a shape `parseCsvSheet` supports on purpose and
#            `capTableCsvParity` pins as one real exports have — puts that
#            newline inside the quotes, so the cut landed mid-quote and the scan
#            spent the rest of the header believing it was quoted text. Every
#            separator after it stopped counting, all three candidates came back
#            zero, and the comma won the tie-break by being first.
#
#            Invisible on a comma file, and on the two this function exists for
#            it is the failure it was written to prevent, restated: a semicolon
#            sheet out of a European Excel, or a tab-delimited paste, read as a
#            single column whose name is the entire header row — mapping to
#            nothing, and the client told their file has no cap table in it. The
#            two are readers of the same bytes and the defect was that they
#            disagreed about where the header ends; it now ends where
#            `parseCsvSheet` says it ends.
#
#            **A workbook could show one cap table and import another.**
#            `readZip` collects entries into a `Map`, so two central-directory
#            records naming the same path resolved to whichever came last, and
#            `readXlsx` read that one as the sheet. An `.xlsx` carrying two
#            `xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml` shows one cap table to whoever opens it
#            and hands a different one to the importer: the upload succeeds, the
#            preview is of the part this reader picked, the analyst's check was
#            of the part their spreadsheet picked, and the sheet that wins
#            becomes the share counts a valuation is built on.
#
#            Which part each reader picks is not a thing to reason about. OPC
#            gives a package exactly one part per name and Excel refuses a
#            package that breaks it, so a duplicate is not a workbook whose
#            meaning has to be chosen between — it is a file that was never one,
#            and it earns the same refusal the encrypted and ZIP64 cases get.
#
#            gap: `findUnstorableText` stops at `MAX_SCAN_DEPTH` and
#            `parseStripeEvent` does not ask `findOverDeepValue` beside it, so a
#            NUL nested past twelve levels of a Stripe envelope still reaches the
#            jsonb column. `nulBytes.ts` frames the depth bound as a request-body
#            rule and says provider payloads "do not come through this door" —
#            true of the door, not of the column. Left as written because the
#            right answer for a webhook is probably to bound the depth rather
#            than refuse the delivery, and that is a decision about what a
#            provider is told rather than a bug fix.
#            gap: `apiKeyGuard`'s own two refusals are still silent — R345's open
