#            tests, and two of the five were found by a census that could not
#            see its own second spelling. The lens that produced four of them is
#            the level contract in `shared/failure.ts`: `warn` promises a retry.
#            Ask of every contained catch *who comes back for this*, and the
#            answer is written on the wrong line in more places than a grep for
#            `catch` will show, because the containment is almost always right.
#
#            **A census that read one of two spellings.**
#            `swallowedFailureCensus` has been green since R120 over a
#            population defined as `.catch(() => …)`. The block form —
#            `try { await api(…) } catch { … }` around the identical discard —
#            was never in it. `ScenariosTab` answered a failed read of the saved
#            bull/base/bear cases with `setSaved(null)`, and the comparison
#            section renders on `saved && saved.scenarios.length > 0`: a 403 or
#            a 503 took the only surface those cases appear on off the page,
#            beside a form still inviting one to be saved. The refresh path is
#            the worse half — `loadSaved` runs after a save that landed, so a
#            save followed by a failed reload made the case the user had just
#            stored vanish, which reads as the save having done nothing. The
#            block half is scoped to the guarded block rather than to everything
#            before the catch: a `JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(…))` guard is
#            a `catch { return null }` too and is not a swallowed *request*
#            failure, and requiring the `try` to contain the call also keeps
#            every key distinct, so no entry hides behind another's `(none)`.
#
#            **A diagnostic that answered a question it did not get to ask.**
#            `migrationLockHolders` returned `[]` from its catch, correctly
#            refusing to fail a boot over a diagnostic. But an empty holder list
#            is not neutral: `MigrationLockTimeoutError` reads it as a *finding*
#            and writes the explanation out — the lock "was released and
#            re-taken repeatedly while this runner waited (several replicas
#            booting at once)". A `pg_locks` read that was refused, timed out,
#            or ran on a dead connection therefore made a failed deploy assert a
#            cause nothing had checked, at the moment somebody is reading it,
#            and the one fact that ends the incident was never named. `[]` still
#            means asked-and-nobody; `null` is the third answer.
